Bound the visual sparse map, the warp cache, and the published path.

Applies to hku-mars/FAST-LIVO2 (verified: the vendored M3DGR copy of the
visual subsystem is identical to upstream apart from one unrelated line in
vio.cpp that computes `border`).

Three unbounded containers, all reachable in normal operation:

1. VIOManager::feat_map. generateVisualMapPoints() only skips a grid cell when
   that cell matched an existing map point in the CURRENT frame (grid_num[i] !=
   TYPE_MAP). Any cell that fails retrieval therefore allocates a fresh
   VisualPoint + Feature every frame, forever, even when the platform is
   stationary and looking at the same scene. Nothing ever erases from
   voxel_points; feat_map is only freed in ~VIOManager.

   The amplifier is Feature::img_, which is assigned the WHOLE frame
   (vio.cpp: `ftr_new->img_ = img;` at both add sites). cv::Mat is refcounted,
   so every retained Feature pins an entire decoded image, not just its patch.
   The set of frames resident in RAM therefore grows monotonically with runtime.

   Measured on an Ouster OS0-64 + OAK-D platform, STATIONARY, 1280x800:
     t=20s  550 voxels   363 MiB
     t=40s  602 voxels   718 MiB
     t=60s  640 voxels  1.06 GiB
     t=80s  676 voxels  1.41 GiB
   Voxel count +23%, memory +288%: the map was not growing in extent, it was
   densifying, and each new point pinned another full frame. ~18 MiB/s, which
   is ~6 frames/s at 3.07 MB per decoded frame. A longer run was OOM-killed at
   21.5 GiB.

   Fix: VIOManager::trimVisualMap(), called at the end of processFrame() once
   visual_submap is no longer read (retrieveFromVisualSparseMap() resets it
   before the next frame, so eviction cannot dangle a borrowed pointer). It
   caps points per voxel, keeping the most recently observed (obs_ is
   push_front-ed, so obs_.front()->id_ is the last frame that saw the point),
   which evicts exactly the create-once-never-matched churn. It also adds a
   position-triggered slide mirroring VoxelMapManager::mapSliding(), which the
   LiDAR map already has and the visual map does not.

2. VIOManager::warp_map is cleared only when normal_en is false. With
   normal_en true (the shipped default) it grows one entry per frame forever.
   It is a cache, so dropping it wholesale is safe.

3. LIVMapper::publish_path appends to path.poses forever AND republishes the
   entire path every frame, so both the retained message and the per-frame
   serialisation cost grow without bound.

Every new knob defaults to the previous unbounded behaviour, so this patch is
a no-op until configured.

